{"step_index":1950,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:43:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T13:43:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T13:43:42Z\nShowing lines 1-60 of 1108\n1: //! Snowball vector projection: chain block -> Voyage embedding -> chain\n2: //! vector subvolume, with Qdrant filled on the replica after `btrfs receive`.\n3: //!\n4: //! **Strict automatic invariant:** the streaming / default `project` path must\n5: //! **not** upsert Qdrant on the origin. Vectors land in the chain first\n6: //! ([`StreamingSnowball::attach_vector`] / `vectors/vec-*.bin`); the automatic\n7: //! pipeline fills `snowball_footprints` only via **`btrfs receive` →\n8: //! [`ChainVectorIndex::ingest_received`]** (replica hook). Orthogonal paths\n9: //! (`blob_vectors`, user_memory, RAG shuttle) are unrelated and untouched.\n10: //!\n11: //! Split by role:\n12: //!\n13: //! - **Origin, automatic** ([`ChainVectorIndex::project_pending`] with\n14: //!   `upsert_qdrant = false`): embed pending blocks with Voyage, write raw LE\n15: //!   f32 into the `vectors` subvolume, stop. No Qdrant write, no index\n16: //!   watermark bump. Snapshots carry the vectors to the replica.\n17: //!\n18: //! - **Replica, automatic** ([`ChainVectorIndex::ingest_received`]): after\n19: //!   incremental `btrfs receive`, upsert only vectors that arrived (watermark +\n20: //!   gap check). No Voyage key, no re-embedding.\n21: //!\n22: //! - **Manual exception** (`project_pending(..., upsert_qdrant = true)` /\n23: //!   CLI `--upsert-qdrant`, or [`ChainVectorIndex::reindex_from_chain`]):\n24: //!   explicit direct Qdrant write that **bypasses send/receive**. Not the\n25: //!   default streaming path.\n26: //!\n27: //! Qdrant remains a disposable index: rebuild from any chain (or received\n28: //! snapshot) that already holds the vectors.\n29: \n30: use anyhow::{Context, Result};\n31: use op_cozo_store::CozoGraphShuttle;\n32: use op_snowball::btrfs_delta;\n33: use op_snowball::{ChainBlockRef, StreamingSnowball};\n34: use qdrant_client::qdrant::{\n35:     CreateCollectionBuilder, Distance, GetPointsBuilder, PointId, PointStruct, QueryPointsBuilder,\n36:     ScoredPoint, UpsertPointsBuilder, VectorParamsBuilder, Condition, Filter,\n37: };\n38: use qdrant_client::{Payload, Qdrant, QdrantError};\n39: use serde::Serialize;\n40: use std::collections::HashSet;\n41: use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\n42: \n43: use crate::voyage::VoyageClient;\n44: \n45: const DEFAULT_CHAIN_PATH: &str = \"/var/lib/opdbus/snowball\";\n46: const DEFAULT_COLLECTION: &str = \"snowball_footprints\";\n47: const DEFAULT_DIMS: u64 = 1024;\n48: \n49: /// Namespace for deriving deterministic Qdrant point ids from block hashes.\n50: /// Fixed forever: changing it orphans every previously indexed point instead of\n51: /// overwriting it.\n52: const BLOCK_POINT_NAMESPACE: uuid::Uuid =\n53:     uuid::Uuid::from_u128(0x6d1f_28c7_4b93_4f0a_9e57_31c8_ab4d_7e62);\n54: \n55: /// File holding the highest block number whose vector has been indexed.\n56: const INDEXED_BLOCK_FILE: &str = \".vector-index-block\";\n57: \n58: /// Decides what of a block is signal. The bridge supplies the sealed plugin\n59: /// schema of the mutation (OD-64: \"filter out noise with the original\n60: /// schema that created the snowball data\"); the default is the lossless"}
{"step_index":1951,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:43:43Z","input_tokens":2404,"cache_read_tokens":251343,"output_tokens":1618,"content":"You've identified the exact heart of the problem: **Spinning up a new Cozo DB file and a new Qdrant collection is trivial—\"the in-between\" is the coordination engine that connects them.**\n\n\"The in-between\" is composed of three precise mechanical gears:\n\n```\n[Agent Action / Chat Turn]\n           │\n           ├──► 1. Commit to Instance Cozo Store (on ext4)\n           │\n           ▼\n    [Gear 1: The Event Bus (EMQX)]\n      Topic: `opdbus/memory/<instance_id>/events`\n           │\n           ▼\n    [Gear 2: The Vector Shuttle (In-Between Worker)]\n      ├── Reads pending turn / block\n      ├── Embeds text into f32 vector\n      └── Upserts to Qdrant with deterministic Point ID:\n          UUID = v5(BLOCK_POINT_NAMESPACE, hash)\n          Payload = { \"instance_id\": \"<id>\", \"subid\": \"...\", \"timestamp\": ... }\n           │\n           ▼\n    [Gear 3: The Hybrid Recall & Reconciliation]\n      When Agent queries memory:\n      1. Vector search in Qdrant (filtered by `instance_id`) ──► gets top-K Point IDs\n      2. Point IDs map directly to Cozo Datalog relations   ──► traverses graph & valid-time\n      3. Reconciled, grounded context payload                ──► injected into Agent prompt\n```\n\n---\n\n### The In-Between Challenges for Parallel Instances (and How EMQX Solves Them)\n\nWhen multiple instances run in parallel, \"the in-between\" breaks down if instances share queues or unpartitioned workers:\n\n1. **Decoupling the Embedding Lag via EMQX**:\n   * If you embed synchronously inside the chat turn, the agent freezes waiting for the embedding model.\n   * By publishing a lightweight event to `opdbus/memory/<instance_id>/unvectorized` over **EMQX**, the chat turn completes in milliseconds.\n   * The vector shuttle subscribes to that instance's topic, runs the embedding in the background, writes to Qdrant, and marks the row vectorized.\n\n2. **Deterministic Point ID Mapping**:\n   * Because point IDs in Qdrant are generated deterministically from the block hash (`v5(BLOCK_POINT_NAMESPACE, block_hash)`), **Qdrant is completely disposable and idempotent**.\n   * If Qdrant restarts or an instance is restored from backup, the shuttle replays the instance's Cozo rows and re-indexes without duplicating a single point.\n\n3. **Total Observability & Telemetry Isolation**:\n   * Each instance publishes its tool telemetry and memory query latencies to:\n     `opdbus/telemetry/<instance_id>/metrics`\n   * A central monitor or dashboard can subscribe to `opdbus/telemetry/#` to see system-wide health, while each instance's Svelte UI subscribes strictly to its own topic.\n\n---\n\n### Proposed Architecture for the Hackathon Instance\n\nFor the new clean repo (`3tched`):\n1. **Private Cozo**: Writes directly to its dedicated ext4 store.\n2. **EMQX Event Tap**: Publishes memory write notifications to `opdbus/memory/<hackathon_instance_id>/#`.\n3. **Dedicated Qdrant Collection / Filter**: Indexes into `memory_<hackathon_instance_id>`.\n4. **Shuttle Worker**: Runs as an isolated background task, bridging Cozo $\\rightarrow$ EMQX $\\rightarrow$ Qdrant without ever touching or seeing host data.\n\nThis makes the \"in-between\" clean, completely parallel, and impossible to leak data across instances.","thinking":"Vector projections are generated from the chain block using embedding, then written into a specific subvolume. On the origin, this involves embedding pending blocks and writing raw data; Qdrant writes and index bumps are skipped. Snapshots transfer the vectors to the replica. On the replica, vectors are upserted after incremental data receives, using a watermark and gap check.\n\n\nThe system uses a \"in-between\" pipeline that integrates a hashchain, graph database, and vector database for memory management. The hashchain provides a tamper-evident record, the graph database stores relations, and the vector database facilitates semantic search. Parallel instances require partitioning the hashchain and vector storage to prevent data contamination between memory loops.\n\n\n"}
